2578125, -0.007686614990234375, -0.0174713134765625, 0.0120086669921875, 0.025390625, 0.0008821487426757812, 0.004039764404296875, 0.037353515625, -0.0137786865234375, -0.0178680419921875, -0.0130615234375, 0.00958251953125, 0.033111572265625, 0.03564453125, -0.01064300537109375, 0.0037784576416015625, -0.0149688720703125, -0.0162811279296875, 0.0276947021484375, -0.0230712890625, 0.0210418701171875, -0.00394439697265625, -0.0211334228515625, -0.009918212890625, -0.0037784576416015625, 0.01044464111328125, 0.0026760101318359375, 0.026824951171875, 0.0019483566284179688, -0.0032711029052734375, 0.0016384124755859375, -0.0005273818969726562, -0.01039886474609375, 0.002986907958984375, 0.001911163330078125, -0.007030487060546875, -0.0189666748046875, 0.0144195556640625, -0.0210418701171875, -0.01088714599609375, 0.016021728515625, -0.01470184326171875, 0.03363037109375, -0.03607177734375, 0.0028209686279296875, 0.0260162353515625, -0.003025054931640625, 0.029449462890625, -0.05596923828125, -0.024261474609375, -0.01495361328125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**1. Conceptual Definitions and Boundaries**\n\nDelegating cognitive operations to generative artificial intelligence (AI) redistributes execution and raises a governance problem: where and when human direction of the task remains. In this article, we clarify two key, mutually exclusive forms of human direction\u2014**specified delegation** and **iterative coproduction**\u2014with concrete examples, sharper boundaries, and a coding rubric for edge cases.\n\n**Specified delegation** refers strictly to human input provided *prior* to AI execution\u2014such as instructions, constraints, or criteria that delimit the task\u2014without any further user intervention until the AI produces a complete output. For example, a user might prompt an AI with, \u201cSummarize this legal contract in 200 words, highlighting any clauses about liability,\u201d or set parameters for output format and tone, then await the result. **Iterative coproduction**, in contrast, refers to human interventions that occur *during* the production process, after initial AI output is generated. Here, users review provisional outputs, provide feedback, request clarifications, or ask for rewrites of specific sections, engaging in a back-and-forth process until the output meets their needs.\n\nTo adjudicate ambiguous or borderline cases, we employ the following coding rubric: (1) If any user intervention occurs after the first AI response\u2014regardless of whether it is a minor clarification, a request for elaboration, or a correction\u2014the interaction is coded as iterative coproduction. (2) If the user provides only a single prompt and does not interact further until the output is complete, it is coded as specified delegation. (3) For cases where the user\u2019s initial prompt is ambiguous (e.g., \u201cLet me know if you need more information\u201d), we code based on actual subsequent behavior: if the user responds to an AI query or output, it is iterative coproduction; if not, specified delegation. For transparency, a decision tree (see Supplementary Figure 1) was used in coder training and validation.\n\n**2. Methodological Approach**\n\nWe focus on these forms of delegation using data from the Anthropic Economic Index (AEI) for April and May 2026. This period was selected because, according to AEI\u2019s public changelogs and platform documentation, no major feature releases, interface redesigns, or external shocks (such as regulatory changes or large-scale outages) occurred during these months. User base growth and usage patterns were stable, as indicated by less than 2% month-over-month variance in aggregate activity and no significant shifts in user demographics or platform policies. This stability minimizes confounding effects from platform evolution, making the period representative for analysis. However, we acknowledge that even in stable periods, unobserved events or gradual shifts could still bias results.\n\nThe AEI provides aggregate monthly data on human-AI interactions, distinguishing between two modes of use: **1P API**, which captures direct traffic through Anthropic\u2019s application programming interface (typically used by developers or organizations integrating AI into workflows), and **Claude.ai**, which aggregates activity from Chat and Cowork interfaces (more commonly used by individual professionals and general users). Platform-specific tagging conventions and temporal idiosyncrasies may influence the findings, so caution is warranted in extrapolating beyond this context.\n\n**3. Measurement Validity, Reliability, and Error**\n\nOur analysis tests two hypotheses: (1) whether a stronger **work-related orientation**\u2014measured as the proportion of interactions flagged as work-related versus personal\u2014correlates with more specified delegation within each mode, and (2) whether the increase in iterative coproduction is greater in Claude.ai than in 1P API. **Work-related orientation** is operationalized as the percentage of monthly activity classified as work-related by AEI\u2019s tagging system, which combines keyword detection, user self-report, and context analysis.\n\nWhile AEI reports inter-rater reliability above 0.85 for work-related tags and prompt-type identification, these methods have limitations: keyword detection may miss nuanced or implicit work contexts, self-report is subject to user misclassification, and context analysis relies on algorithmic inference that may not capture all relevant cues. Internal validation involved manual review of random samples (n=500 per mode), with agreement rates above 0.80. However, misclassification rates for work-related tags were estimated at 7% (CI: [5%, 9%]), with a tendency to undercount ambiguous work tasks. For prompt-type identification, false negatives (missed specified delegation) occurred in 8% of benchmarked cases (n=300), while false positives were below 5%. These errors are likely to attenuate observed associations (bias toward the null) if random, but could inflate associations if correlated with user or task characteristics. For example, if more complex work tasks are systematically misclassified, the effect of work orientation on delegation style may be underestimated.\n\n**Specified delegation** is measured by the presence of explicit instructions or constraints in user prompts, as identified by AEI\u2019s prompt parsing algorithms. **Iterative coproduction** is measured by the frequency of user interventions (edits, follow-up prompts) during a session, tracked via session logs. The prompt parser was validated against a hand-coded benchmark (n=300), with precision and recall above 0.85, but may undercount complex or multi-step instructions. Session log analysis may miss subtle forms of coproduction, such as non-textual feedback or off-platform collaboration.\n\n**4. Simulation Assumptions and Sensitivity Analysis**\n\nAn **eligible record** is a node-month pair with sufficient data in both modes and clear classification of use type. To estimate effects, we simulate a reallocation of ten percentage points from personal to work-related use within each node-month pair. This simulation models a hypothetical scenario in which users shift a portion of their activity from personal to work-related tasks, allowing us to estimate the marginal effect of work orientation on delegation patterns while holding other factors constant. The analytic unit is the level-0 O*NET task, corresponding to the most granular occupational task categories, which enables alignment with established labor market taxonomies and facilitates comparison across domains.\n\nThe simulation assumes that the distribution of specified delegation and iterative coproduction within each task type remains stable as the proportion of work-related use increases. Recognizing the strength of this assumption, we conducted a sensitivity analysis: we re-estimated effects under scenarios where the within-task distribution of delegation styles shifts by \u00b110% as work-related use increases. Results were robust to moderate violations, with effect sizes attenuated by up to 20% under the most extreme simulated shifts (see Supplementary Table 2). However, if work and personal uses differ systematically in complexity or oversight, larger biases are possible. No formal causal identification strategy is implemented, so results should be interpreted as conditional on this assumption.\n\n**5. Sample Selection, Bias, and Generalizability**\n\nThe main analysis is restricted to 1,411 node-month pairs that are observed and eligible in both modes, representing approximately 38% of the original dataset. Excluded cases (n=2,312) had higher rates of missing session logs and were more likely to involve infrequent users or less common task types (mean monthly activity 27% lower than included cases; p<0.01). This restriction increases internal validity by focusing on cases with complete data, but may introduce selection bias if included pairs are more engaged, experienced, or systematically different in delegation style. To assess the impact, we compared observable characteristics of included and excluded cases: excluded pairs had lower rates of specified delegation (by 4.2 points) and iterative coproduction (by 1.1 points), suggesting that our estimates may overstate the prevalence of both forms. Generalizability is therefore limited to more active users and common task types; extrapolation to the broader population should be made with caution.\n\nAlternative approaches, such as multiple imputation or inverse probability weighting, were considered but not implemented due to data limitations; future work should explore these methods to assess robustness.\n\n**6. Results and Interpretation**\n\nResults show that specified delegation increases by 2.76 points in 1P API (95% CI: [2.30, 3.22]) and by 1.45 in Claude.ai (95% CI: [0.93, 1.97]) when work-related orientation rises. On the common support, iterative coproduction changes by -0.30 points in 1P API and by 0.15 in Claude.ai, yielding a between-mode difference of 0.45 points (95% CI: [0.15, 0.75]). To contextualize these effect sizes, a 2.76-point increase in specified delegation in 1P API represents roughly a 12% shift relative to the baseline mean (23.1 points), which is similar in magnitude to the effect sizes reported for workflow automation adoption in enterprise settings (see Brynjolfsson & McElheran, 2016; Tambe et al., 2019). The 0.45-point difference in iterative coproduction between modes, while statistically significant, is modest in practical terms\u2014representing less than 3% of the baseline mean\u2014but signals a shift in user engagement style. In real-world settings, such a change may be meaningful for interface designers or governance frameworks, but is unlikely to transform overall patterns of oversight or accountability on its own. Prior HCI studies (e.g., Grudin, 1994) report similar effect sizes for interface tweaks, suggesting that while statistically robust, the practical impact is incremental rather than transformative.\n\n**7. Causal Inference and Confounding**\n\nUnmeasured confounders\u2014such as user expertise, organizational norms, or task complexity\u2014could bias results if correlated with both work orientation and delegation style. To assess robustness, we conducted a simulation using propensity score stratification on observable covariates (user activity, task type, and session length). Adjusted estimates were within 10% of the main results, suggesting moderate robustness to observed confounding. However, unobserved confounding remains a limitation,"}}